**1.3 Engineering problem register.** These are the problems that most often break an app like this. For every row, state your **decision**, the **implementation approach**, and the **test that proves it**.

| ID | Problem | Why it bites here | Solve it by |
|---|---|---|---|
| E1 | Offline check-in and sync | Patchy signal; a lost or duplicated check-in means lost or double pay | Local queue (IndexedDB); client-generated idempotency key per event; server de-duplicates; record device time AND server receive time and flag drift above a configurable limit; visible "saved on phone, will send" status; retry with backoff |
| E2 | Time correctness | Shifts cross midnight; overtime is daily and weekly; device clocks are wrong | Store UTC, display in organisation timezone; derive the work day from shift start, not calendar date; inject a clock into all time-dependent code so tests control it |
| E3 | Money correctness | Floating-point rounding destroys trust in pay | Integer minor units (paise); one configurable rounding function; payslips freeze their inputs, rule version, and result; corrections are adjustment lines |
| E4 | Rule engines (pay, fatigue, risk) | Rules differ by sector and change over time; people must know why a number is what it is | Pure functions taking (data, config) and returning (result, explanation trace); effective-dated config versions; one shared evaluation style for all three engines |
| E5 | Login without email, for low literacy | Email and password do not fit this audience | Admin-provisioned accounts; worker ID + short PIN or QR badge login; lockout and rate limits; PIN reset by a supervisor with an audit entry; stronger credentials for admin roles; shared-device mode where a supervisor's device scans worker badges (also serves workers without smartphones); configurable session timeout |
| E6 | Tenant and worker isolation | One organisation or worker must never see another's data | Organisation id on every table; all queries scoped through one data-access layer; RBAC plus row-level scope (own data, own team, whole organisation); tests that attempt cross-tenant and cross-worker reads and must fail |
| E7 | Anonymity vs duplicate votes | Feedback must be untraceable yet one person should not vote twice | Separate participation record; no join key to responses; bucketed timestamps; small-group and differencing suppression; never feeds per-person scores |
| E8 | Voice and language limits | Speech support varies by browser, language, and connectivity; Tamil and Hindi voices may be missing on cheap phones | Detect capability at runtime per language; offer voice only where it works; otherwise icon + text; document tested browsers; never promise voice that is unavailable |
| E9 | Low-end performance | ~2 GB RAM phones on slow 3G | Code-split; lightweight SVG charts instead of a charting library; pagination; on-device image compression; service worker with cache-first shell and a safe update flow that never strands users on an old version |
| E10 | Media uploads | Photos and audio are large, risky, and can leak location in metadata | Compress on device; strip EXIF; server checks size and real file type (not extension); store outside the web root under generated names; serve only through an authorised endpoint |
| E11 | Configuration integrity | Free-form settings can silently break pay or privacy | Registry validation; sanity ranges; locked principles; effective dating; cache invalidation on change; a migration path when registry keys change; simulate before apply |
| E12 | Internationalisation | Hard-coded strings, longer translated text, plural and number formats, missing keys | Key-based strings; fallback chain; locale formatting; layouts that tolerate longer text; completeness-check script; flags for translations needing native review |
| E13 | Data lifecycle | Demos need data; real use needs retention and deletion | Migrations; idempotent seed; one-command demo reset; configurable retention; worker data export; soft delete with audit |
| E14 | Testing rule-heavy, time-dependent code | Pay and fatigue bugs are costly and subtle | Table-driven unit tests per engine; API integration tests including RBAC; one end-to-end smoke test per sector; deterministic seed and injected clock |
| E15 | Failure experience | Weak connectivity plus low literacy turns errors into abandonment | Errors in the worker's language with icon and next step; never lose typed input; clear offline indicator; retry without re-entering data |
| E16 | Security hardening | A single weak spot undermines everything | Strong password and PIN hashing; CSP and security headers; CSRF protection; parameterised queries; output encoding; rate limits; secrets in environment; append-only audit log; minimal dependencies |
| E17 | Integration risk and scope creep | Big-bang builds fail late | Vertical slices; chunk manifest; app runnable after every chunk; a cut-list |

**1.4 Customisation architecture.** Specify all of the following.

*a) Settings registry.* One typed schema as the single source of truth. Each entry has: key · type · default · allowed range · scope (system, sector preset, organisation, site, or worker) · roles allowed to edit · feature affected · i18n label and help-text keys · risk level (low, medium, high). The registry drives (i) server-side validation, (ii) a generated admin settings screen, (iii) an auto-generated CUSTOMIZATION.md, and (iv) the sector presets. **No setting may exist outside it.**

*b) Resolution order.* System default → sector preset → organisation → site → worker (worker scope only for personal-preference keys). Show one worked example.

*c) Minimum customisable surface.*

| Area | Must be configurable |
|---|---|
| Identity and branding | Organisation name; logo; primary colour (auto-checked for contrast); terminology map (worker/staff/employee, site/campus/ward, shift/period/duty, foreman/HOD/supervisor) |
| Roles and access | Role names; permission matrix within safe limits; who sees the quit-risk list; who sees which dashboards |
| Languages | Languages enabled per organisation; default language; per-worker override; fallback chain |
| Shifts and attendance | Shift templates (start, end, break, grace minutes); weekly off days; holiday calendar; pay period; enabled check-in methods (worker phone, supervisor-held device, manual with audit); geofence radius; QR rotation interval; missed-check-out rule; offline sync window; time rounding |
| Pay | Named pay components (base, overtime, allowances, deductions); overtime multipliers; daily and weekly thresholds; rounding; currency; payslip wording; effective-dated rule versions |
| Fatigue | Max daily and weekly hours; minimum rest between shifts; max consecutive days; night-shift handling; severity bands; who is alerted; nudge text |
| Safety | Hazard categories and icons per sector; severity levels; status stages; response targets per severity; escalation ladder; whether anonymous reports, photos, and voice are allowed |
| Feedback | Question sets per sector; rating style (3 or 5 points, shapes or faces); cycle frequency; minimum-response threshold (above the hard floor); free text on or off |
| Risk heuristic | Factors; weights; thresholds; labels; time window |
| Dashboard | Widgets shown and their order; date ranges; KPI definitions; export formats |
| Notifications | Events that trigger in-app messages; quiet hours |
| Privacy | Retention periods; consent text; worker "download my data" |

*d) Worker personalisation (Layer B).* Language · text size (4 steps) · high-contrast mode · light or dark theme · **Simple mode** (icons and voice only, at most 4 home tiles) versus Standard mode · read-aloud on or off and speed · sound and vibration off · home-tile order · notification preferences · a "What is recorded about me" page. An organisation may set defaults but may **never** force-override a worker's accessibility settings.

*e) Developer extension (Layer C).* Adding a language = one file plus one registration line. Adding a sector = one preset file. Adding a hazard set = one data file. Adding a feature module = one folder with a manifest (id, routes, permissions, settings keys, nav entry). Explain each in plain language and show the manifest format.

*f) Presets.* "Construction Site" and "College/Campus" ship as data, with apply-preset, start-from-preset-then-edit, reset-to-preset, and export/import as validated JSON (rejected if it violates the locked principles).

*g) Safe change management.* (i) Preview and simulate before applying, for example a pay-rule change recalculates the last pay period for sample workers and shows before and after. (ii) Every change is versioned with an effective date; history is never rewritten. (iii) Every change is audit-logged (who, what, old to new, when). (iv) Workers get an in-app notice when a pay, fatigue, or privacy setting that affects them changes. (v) One-click rollback.

*h) Locked principles (no setting can override these).*
1. The anonymity threshold has a hard floor of 3 responses (default 5).
2. A worker can always see their own hours, payslip formula, and everything recorded about them.
3. Location is read only at the moment of check-in, never continuously.
4. Anonymous feedback never feeds any per-person score or view.
5. Payslip formula sections cannot be hidden.
6. Limits are enforced on the server; invalid configurations are rejected with plain-language errors.
7. Every configuration change is audit-logged.

Other values get sanity ranges: a value outside the recommended range triggers a warning and a typed confirmation. Never state a legal limit as fact; say rates and limits must be set per applicable local labour law.

